        if (!blogPostRepository.existsBySlug("failing-online-class-what-to-do")) {
            posts.add(BlogPost.builder()
                .title("Failing an Online Class? Here Is Exactly What To Do Right Now")
                .slug("failing-online-class-what-to-do")
                .excerpt("Falling behind in an online class feels overwhelming, but there are clear steps you can take immediately to recover your grade. Here is the actionable plan that works.")
                .content("<h2>You Are Not Alone — And It Is Not Too Late</h2> <p>Falling behind in an online class is far more common than most students realize. Online courses have higher withdrawal and failure rates than in-person equivalents precisely because the self-directed nature creates unique vulnerabilities. If you are currently failing or at serious risk, here is exactly what to do.</p> <h2>Step 1: Calculate Your Current Standing Immediately</h2> <p>Log into your LMS grade center right now. Add up every missing assignment, its point value, and its weight in the final grade. Identify which upcoming assignments carry the most weight. This arithmetic tells you whether recovery is mathematically possible with remaining coursework.</p> <h2>Step 2: Contact Your Instructor Today</h2> <p>Email your instructor directly. Keep it brief, professional, and honest: you have had personal circumstances affecting your engagement, you are committed to completing the course, and you want to discuss your options. Ask specifically whether late work is accepted with a penalty and whether an incomplete grade is available.</p> <h2>Step 3: Identify the Withdrawal Deadline</h2> <p>Most US universities allow course withdrawal until approximately 60 to 70 percent through the semester. Withdrawing before the deadline means a W on your transcript instead of an F. A W has zero GPA impact. An F has lasting GPA impact and may trigger financial aid repayment requirements.</p> <h2>Step 4: Prioritize High-Weight Remaining Work</h2> <p>If staying in the course, focus exclusively on assignments that carry the most weight. A discussion post worth 1 percent is not where your energy goes. A final exam worth 25 to 30 percent is. Map every remaining assignment by grade weight and focus on the highest-stakes items first.</p> <h2>Step 5: Get Immediate Expert Help</h2> <p>If the volume of catch-up work is beyond what you can complete alone in the available time, professional academic support can help you recover strategically. Our experts take over your class immediately — handling all remaining assignments, discussion posts, and the final exam — giving you a genuine path to passing.</p> <h2>What Never To Do When Failing</h2> <ul><li>Do not disappear entirely from the course — continued absence accelerates failure</li><li>Do not miss the withdrawal deadline trying to salvage a mathematically unrecoverable grade</li><li>Do not ignore financial aid implications — failing courses can trigger repayment requirements</li><li>Do not assume there is nothing you can do — there almost always is, if you act immediately</li></ul>")
                .category("Study Tips")
                .author("EduPilotHelp Team")
                .readTimeMinutes(7)
                .published(true)
                .featured(false)
                .build());
        }
